Eva Amson, even though being isolated is new for many of us, there have always been groups of the population who live with limited social contacts.

63
00:13:43,211 --> 00:13:49,932
Think of prisoners, for example, or patients recovering from medical procedures that require a period in isolation.

64
00:13:50,388 --> 00:13:58,388
These groups have been well studied and researchers know quite a lot about how people handle when they're spending a large amount of time on their own.

65
00:13:58,388 --> 00:14:07,388
One thing that seems to help is art therapy. Art therapy is a form of psychotherapy that uses the creative arts as a therapy medium.

66
00:14:07,388 --> 00:14:15,388
Instead of talking for an hour, you might be painting or sculpting or otherwise expressing your thoughts through a creative medium.

67
00:14:15,827 --> 00:14:25,827
As a form of guided therapy, art therapy is well studied. Research showed that it decreased depression among inmates and made them feel more in control of their own lives.

68
00:14:26,707 --> 00:14:45,507
It relieved anxiety and depression among refugee children, cancer patients undergoing chemotherapy, tuberculosis patients in isolation, people undergoing stem cell transplants or bone marrow transplants, and several other situations where a change in circumstance and increased isolation affected people's mental health.

69
00:14:47,604 --> 00:15:04,244
Art therapy isn't a miraculous cure, its effects on dementia are still debatable for example, but its use to combat depression in social isolation is well documented, and it may just help some people currently struggling with being in lockdown. 222 words.

